/*
  protocol/init.spthy -- PROTOCOL LAYER, participant initialization for secure_email (C1).

  Two parties, both routed through the human layer (multi-party plan: every participant is
  mask-capable). Mirrors alex_blake_kdf/protocol/init.spthy: each Init deposits the persistent
  read-only handle !Party(p,id) for the per-participant stress-enable (protocol/stress_*.spthy),
  and Start(p) keys OneInstancePerHuman (core/types.spthy) so each identity is ONE person.

  Alex = sender. Blake = recipient. The recipient comes in TWO mutually-exclusive flavours --
  this is the "users can choose between two ways of sending" branch from the guide:

    * KEYED recipient  (Method 2 / PGP): Blake owns a key pair ~skB and PUBLISHES pk(~skB) to the
      world (keyserver / WKD -- so it lands on the public network via Out, and the Dolev-Yao
      adversary sees it, as a real keyserver entry would be). !RecipientKey carries the genuine key.
    * KEYLESS recipient (Method 3 / password): Blake has no keys (!Keyless). The sender must fall
      back to a password.

  Both Init_Recipient_* are keyed Start('Blake'), so OneInstancePerHuman picks exactly one flavour
  per trace; the exists-trace lemmas explore each pathway, all-traces lemmas quantify over both.
*/

rule Init_Sender:
    [ Fr(~idA) ]
  --[ Start('Alex') ]->
    [ AlexInit(~idA), !Party('Alex', ~idA) ]

rule Init_Recipient_PGP:
    [ Fr(~idB), Fr(~skB) ]
  --[ Start('Blake'), RecipientKeyed('Blake') ]->
    [ BlakeKeyed(~idB, ~skB), !Party('Blake', ~idB),
      !RecipientKey('Blake', pk(~skB)), Out(pk(~skB)) ]

rule Init_Recipient_NoKey:
    [ Fr(~idB) ]
  --[ Start('Blake'), RecipientKeyless('Blake') ]->
    [ BlakeKeyless(~idB), !Party('Blake', ~idB), !Keyless('Blake') ]
